// PRISM operational model of <algorithm>. Partial on purpose.
// The pir stage puts this directory first on the C++ include path, so
// `#include <algorithm>` reaches this file instead of libstdc++'s. It is a
// file, not a directory: a directory of this name would make the include
// fail with EISDIR and every such unit would fall back to libstdc++.
//
// std::min, std::max, and std::clamp, for a type comparable with operator<.
// min(a, b) returns a when !(b < a), otherwise b. Equal arguments return
// the first argument. max is the symmetric rule: a when !(a < b), otherwise
// b. The result is one of those two arguments. It is not a third value, and
// it is not a sum that wrapped to 0.
//
// clamp(v, lo, hi): when hi < lo, __prism_check with class FUNC-CONTRACT
// and then __builtin_trap. That path does not return v. When the range is
// valid, the result is lo if v < lo, hi if hi < v, otherwise v.
//
// std::min_element and std::max_element take two pointers, first and last.
// There is no comparator. When last < first, __prism_check with class
// FUNC-CONTRACT and then __builtin_trap. That path does not return a
// pointer. When first == last, the result is last. That path does not read
// an element and does not invent a T. Otherwise the result is a pointer to
// the minimum or the maximum element in [first, last). Equal elements keep
// the first.
//
// std::find takes two pointers, first and last, and a const T& value.
// There is no other overload. When last < first, __prism_check with class
// FUNC-CONTRACT and then __builtin_trap. That path does not read an element
// and does not invent a T. When first == last, the result is last. That
// path does not read an element. Otherwise the result is a pointer to the
// first element in [first, last) that compares equal with operator==, or
// last if none. The function does not allocate.
//
// std::find_if takes two pointers and one function pointer,
// bool (*)(const T&). There is no other overload. A null predicate is
// __prism_check with class FUNC-CONTRACT and then __builtin_trap. That
// path does not call the predicate. When last < first, __prism_check with
// class FUNC-CONTRACT and the message "find_if: last < first", then
// __builtin_trap. That path does not read an element and does not invent
// a T. When first == last, the result is last. That path does not read
// an element and does not call the predicate. Otherwise the result is a
// pointer to the first element for which the predicate returns true, or
// last if none. The function does not allocate.
//
// std::count takes two pointers and a const T&. The result is a ptrdiff_t:
// how many elements in [first, last) compare equal with operator==. When
// last < first, __prism_check with class FUNC-CONTRACT and then
// __builtin_trap. That path does not read an element. When first == last,
// the result is 0. That path does not read an element. A count that would
// not fit in ptrdiff_t is __prism_check with class FUNC-CONTRACT and then
// __builtin_trap. That path does not return a wrapped count. The function
// does not allocate.
//
// std::all_of, std::any_of, and std::none_of each take two pointers and
// one callable predicate. There is one overload of each name. When the
// predicate is a pointer (a function pointer) and it is null, the call is
// __prism_check with class FUNC-CONTRACT and the message
// "all_of: null predicate" (and the same shape for any_of and none_of),
// then __builtin_trap. That path does not call the predicate. A callable
// that is not a pointer (a lambda, a function object) has no null state
// and is not checked for one. When last < first, __prism_check with class
// FUNC-CONTRACT and the message "all_of: last < first" (same shape for the
// others), then __builtin_trap. That path does not read an element. When
// first == last, the function does not read and does not call: all_of
// returns true, any_of returns false, and none_of returns true
// ([alg.all.of]). Otherwise all_of returns false at the first element for
// which the predicate is false, else true. any_of returns true at the
// first element for which it is true, else false. none_of returns false
// at the first element for which it is true, else true. The functions do
// not allocate.
//
// The other C++11 predicates over two pointers and any callable
// predicate: find_if_not, copy_if, is_partitioned, partition_point,
// minmax, minmax_element, is_heap, is_heap_until. An inverted range is
// __prism_check FUNC-CONTRACT then __builtin_trap, as above. A null
// predicate is not checked by these. minmax_element returns the first
// smallest and the last largest.
//
// std::fill takes two pointers and a const T&. There is no other overload.
// When last < first, __prism_check with class FUNC-CONTRACT and the message
// "std::fill last is before first", then __builtin_trap. That path does not
// write. When first == last, the function returns. That path does not write,
// even when the pointer is null. A null first on a non-empty range is
// __prism_check with class PTR-NULL-DEREF and the message
// "std::fill null destination", then __builtin_trap. That path does not
// write. Otherwise each element in [first, last) is assigned the value.
// The element at last is not written. The function does not allocate.
//
// std::copy takes two pointers, first and last, and a destination pointer.
// There is no other overload. When last < first, __prism_check with class
// FUNC-CONTRACT and the message "std::copy last is before first", then
// __builtin_trap. That path does not read an element and does not write.
// When first == last, the result is dest. That path does not read and does
// not write, even when dest is null. A null dest on a non-empty range is
// __prism_check with class PTR-NULL-DEREF and the message
// "std::copy null destination", then __builtin_trap. That path does not
// write. When dest lies in [first, last), __prism_check with class
// FUNC-CONTRACT and the message "std::copy dest lies in [first, last)",
// then __builtin_trap. That path does not write. The overlap scan compares
// pointers with equality and does not read an element. Otherwise each
// element in [first, last) is assigned through the destination, in order.
// The element at last is not written. The result is the destination one
// past the last element written. The function does not allocate.
//
// std::copy_n takes one pointer, a count of type std::size_t, and a
// destination pointer. There is no other overload. When n == 0, the result
// is dest. That path does not read and does not write, even when dest or
// the source is null. A null source with n > 0 is __prism_check with class
// PTR-NULL-DEREF and the message "std::copy_n null source", then
// __builtin_trap. That path does not read. A null dest with n > 0 is
// __prism_check with class PTR-NULL-DEREF and the message
// "std::copy_n null destination", then __builtin_trap. That path does not
// write. Otherwise n elements are assigned through the destination, in
// order. The element one past the last element written is not written.
// The result is the destination one past the last element written. The
// function does not allocate.
//
// std::copy_backward takes three pointers, first, last, and dest. There is
// no other overload. A const source and a non-const dest do not match.
// When last < first, __prism_check with class FUNC-CONTRACT and the
// message "std::copy_backward last is before first", then __builtin_trap.
// That path does not read an element and does not write. When
// first == last, the result is dest. That path does not read and does not
// write, even when dest is null. That empty check is before the
// null-destination check. A null dest on a non-empty range is
// __prism_check with class PTR-NULL-DEREF and the message
// "std::copy_backward null destination", then __builtin_trap. That path
// does not write. When dest lies in (first, last], __prism_check with
// class FUNC-CONTRACT and the message
// "std::copy_backward dest lies in (first, last]", then __builtin_trap
// ([alg.copy]). That path does not write. The overlap scan compares
// pointers with equality and does not read an element. Otherwise the
// elements in [first, last) are copied from the back into the positions
// ending at dest. dest is not written. The element at last is not read.
// The result is the start of the written range. The function does not
// allocate.
//
// std::ranges::count_if is declared here, as in libstdc++, where it is in
// this header and not in the ranges header. It takes a T(&)[N] and a
// function pointer bool (*)(const T&). The result is how many elements make
// the predicate true, as a ptrdiff_t. A null predicate is __prism_check with
// class FUNC-CONTRACT and the message "std::ranges::count_if null
// predicate", then __builtin_trap. That path does not call the predicate
// and does not load an element. The subscript is an index below N. N == 0
// does not match, and a zero-length array matches no overload: libstdc++ 14
// under clang 23 rejects that call too. A pointer, an
// iterator pair, a class range, a projection, and a predicate that takes T
// by value do not match, so those calls fail to compile and pir falls back.
// std::count_if, the iterator form, is not declared.
//
// sort, for_each, and every other algorithm are not declared. A use of
// those names fails to compile and the pir stage falls back to libstdc++
// (extra.cxx_models). A partial header is intentional.
//
// This header is not a proof that a range is sorted. It is not PROVED
// and it is not CLEAN. After a failed check the path traps. It does not
// continue into a read, a write, or a fabricated success.
#ifndef _GLIBCXX_ALGORITHM
#define _GLIBCXX_ALGORITHM 1

#pragma GCC system_header

#include <bits/stl_pair.h>

// PIR intrinsic (translate.cpp). Literal class and message, as in the libc
// models. Not defined here: the analysed program does not link a body.
extern "C" void __prism_check(int ok, const char* cls, const char* msg);

namespace std {

// Same typedef as bits/c++config.h. This header includes nothing. The count
// parameter of copy_n is this std::size_t. A later header may repeat the
// typedef for the same type.
typedef __SIZE_TYPE__ size_t;

template <typename _Tp>
concept _Prism_less_than_comparable = requires(const _Tp& __x, const _Tp& __y) { __x < __y; };

template <_Prism_less_than_comparable _Tp>
constexpr const _Tp& min(const _Tp& __a, const _Tp& __b) {
    // Equal arguments: !(b < a), so the first argument is returned.
    if (!(__b < __a)) return __a;
    return __b;
}

template <_Prism_less_than_comparable _Tp>
constexpr const _Tp& max(const _Tp& __a, const _Tp& __b) {
    if (!(__a < __b)) return __a;
    return __b;
}

template <_Prism_less_than_comparable _Tp>
constexpr const _Tp& clamp(const _Tp& __v, const _Tp& __lo, const _Tp& __hi) {
    if (__hi < __lo) {
        __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "clamp: hi < lo");
        // The range is not valid. __v is not a result of this path.
        __builtin_trap();
    }
    if (__v < __lo) return __lo;
    if (__hi < __v) return __hi;
    return __v;
}

// Two pointers only. An inverted range traps. An empty range returns last
// and does not read an element. Equal elements keep the earlier pointer.
template <_Prism_less_than_comparable _Tp>
constexpr _Tp* min_element(_Tp* __first, _Tp* __last) {
    if (__last < __first) {
        __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "min_element: last < first");
        __builtin_trap();
    }
    if (__first == __last) return __last;
    _Tp* __result = __first;
    ++__first;
    while (__first != __last) {
        if (*__first < *__result) __result = __first;
        ++__first;
    }
    return __result;
}

template <_Prism_less_than_comparable _Tp>
constexpr _Tp* max_element(_Tp* __first, _Tp* __last) {
    if (__last < __first) {
        __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "max_element: last < first");
        __builtin_trap();
    }
    if (__first == __last) return __last;
    _Tp* __result = __first;
    ++__first;
    while (__first != __last) {
        if (*__result < *__first) __result = __first;
        ++__first;
    }
    return __result;
}

template <typename _Tp>
concept _Prism_equality_comparable = requires(const _Tp& __x, const _Tp& __y) { __x == __y; };

// Two pointers and one value. An inverted range traps without reading and
// without inventing a T. An empty range returns last and does not read.
// operator== picks the first match. Nothing is allocated.
template <_Prism_equality_comparable _Tp>
constexpr _Tp* find(_Tp* __first, _Tp* __last, const _Tp& __value) {
    if (__last < __first) {
        __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "find: last < first");
        __builtin_trap();
    }
    if (__first == __last) return __last;
    while (__first != __last) {
        if (*__first == __value) return __first;
        ++__first;
    }
    return __last;
}

// Function pointer only. A null predicate traps before any call. An
// inverted range traps before any read. An empty range returns last and
// does not call. This is not PROVED and it is not CLEAN.
template <typename _Tp>
constexpr _Tp* find_if(_Tp* __first, _Tp* __last, bool (*__pred)(const _Tp&)) {
    if (__pred == nullptr) {
        __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "find_if: null predicate");
        __builtin_trap();
    }
    if (__last < __first) {
        __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "find_if: last < first");
        __builtin_trap();
    }
    if (__first == __last) return __last;
    while (__first != __last) {
        if (__pred(*__first)) return __first;
        ++__first;
    }
    return __last;
}

// Two pointers and one value. An inverted range traps without reading.
// An empty range returns 0 and does not read. The tally is a ptrdiff_t.
// One more match than that type can hold traps and does not wrap.
// This is not PROVED and it is not CLEAN.
template <_Prism_equality_comparable _Tp>
constexpr __PTRDIFF_TYPE__ count(_Tp* __first, _Tp* __last, const _Tp& __value) {
    if (__last < __first) {
        __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "count: last < first");
        __builtin_trap();
    }
    if (__first == __last) return 0;
    __PTRDIFF_TYPE__ __n = 0;
    while (__first != __last) {
        if (*__first == __value) {
            if (__n == static_cast<__PTRDIFF_TYPE__>(__PTRDIFF_MAX__)) {
                __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "count: result does not fit in ptrdiff_t");
                __builtin_trap();
            }
            ++__n;
        }
        ++__first;
    }
    return __n;
}

#define _PRISM_RANGE_CHECK(__f, __l, __name)                                   \
    if ((__l) < (__f)) {                                                       \
        __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", __name ": last < first");            \
        __builtin_trap();                                                      \
    }

// Any callable. A null function pointer traps before any call. An
// inverted range traps before any read. An empty range does not call and
// returns true. Otherwise false at the first element for which the
// predicate is false, else true. This is not PROVED and it is not CLEAN.
template <typename _Tp, typename _Pred>
constexpr bool all_of(_Tp* __first, _Tp* __last, _Pred __pred) {
    if constexpr (__is_pointer(_Pred)) {
        if (__pred == nullptr) {
            __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "all_of: null predicate");
            __builtin_trap();
        }
    }
    if (__last < __first) {
        __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "all_of: last < first");
        __builtin_trap();
    }
    if (__first == __last) return true;
    while (__first != __last) {
        if (!__pred(*__first)) return false;
        ++__first;
    }
    return true;
}
// Any callable. A null function pointer traps before any call. An
// inverted range traps before any read. An empty range does not call and
// returns false. Otherwise true at the first element for which the
// predicate is true, else false. This is not PROVED and it is not CLEAN.
template <typename _Tp, typename _Pred>
constexpr bool any_of(_Tp* __first, _Tp* __last, _Pred __pred) {
    if constexpr (__is_pointer(_Pred)) {
        if (__pred == nullptr) {
            __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "any_of: null predicate");
            __builtin_trap();
        }
    }
    if (__last < __first) {
        __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "any_of: last < first");
        __builtin_trap();
    }
    if (__first == __last) return false;
    while (__first != __last) {
        if (__pred(*__first)) return true;
        ++__first;
    }
    return false;
}
// Any callable. A null function pointer traps before any call. An
// inverted range traps before any read. An empty range does not call and
// returns true. Otherwise false at the first element for which the
// predicate is true, else true. This is not PROVED and it is not CLEAN.
template <typename _Tp, typename _Pred>
constexpr bool none_of(_Tp* __first, _Tp* __last, _Pred __pred) {
    if constexpr (__is_pointer(_Pred)) {
        if (__pred == nullptr) {
            __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "none_of: null predicate");
            __builtin_trap();
        }
    }
    if (__last < __first) {
        __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "none_of: last < first");
        __builtin_trap();
    }
    if (__first == __last) return true;
    while (__first != __last) {
        if (__pred(*__first)) return false;
        ++__first;
    }
    return true;
}
template <typename _Tp, typename _Pred>
constexpr _Tp* find_if_not(_Tp* __first, _Tp* __last, _Pred __pred) {
    _PRISM_RANGE_CHECK(__first, __last, "find_if_not")
    for (; __first != __last; ++__first)
        if (!__pred(*__first)) return __first;
    return __last;
}
template <typename _Tp, typename _Up, typename _Pred>
constexpr _Up* copy_if(_Tp* __first, _Tp* __last, _Up* __out, _Pred __pred) {
    _PRISM_RANGE_CHECK(__first, __last, "copy_if")
    for (; __first != __last; ++__first)
        if (__pred(*__first)) *__out++ = *__first;
    return __out;
}
template <typename _Tp, typename _Pred>
constexpr bool is_partitioned(_Tp* __first, _Tp* __last, _Pred __pred) {
    _PRISM_RANGE_CHECK(__first, __last, "is_partitioned")
    for (; __first != __last && __pred(*__first); ++__first) {}
    for (; __first != __last; ++__first)
        if (__pred(*__first)) return false;
    return true;
}
// Linear scan: the first element for which pred is false. On a range that
// is_partitioned this is the partition point required by [alg.partitions].
template <typename _Tp, typename _Pred>
constexpr _Tp* partition_point(_Tp* __first, _Tp* __last, _Pred __pred) {
    _PRISM_RANGE_CHECK(__first, __last, "partition_point")
    for (; __first != __last; ++__first)
        if (!__pred(*__first)) return __first;
    return __last;
}
template <_Prism_less_than_comparable _Tp>
constexpr pair<const _Tp&, const _Tp&> minmax(const _Tp& __a, const _Tp& __b) {
    if (__b < __a) return pair<const _Tp&, const _Tp&>(__b, __a);
    return pair<const _Tp&, const _Tp&>(__a, __b);
}
template <_Prism_less_than_comparable _Tp>
constexpr pair<_Tp*, _Tp*> minmax_element(_Tp* __first, _Tp* __last) {
    _PRISM_RANGE_CHECK(__first, __last, "minmax_element")
    _Tp* __lo = __first;
    _Tp* __hi = __first;
    for (_Tp* __p = __first; __p != __last; ++__p) {
        if (*__p < *__lo) __lo = __p;
        if (!(*__p < *__hi)) __hi = __p;  // last largest
    }
    return pair<_Tp*, _Tp*>(__lo, __hi);
}
template <_Prism_less_than_comparable _Tp>
constexpr _Tp* is_heap_until(_Tp* __first, _Tp* __last) {
    _PRISM_RANGE_CHECK(__first, __last, "is_heap_until")
    const __PTRDIFF_TYPE__ __n = __last - __first;
    for (__PTRDIFF_TYPE__ __i = 1; __i < __n; ++__i)
        if (__first[(__i - 1) / 2] < __first[__i]) return __first + __i;
    return __last;
}
template <_Prism_less_than_comparable _Tp>
constexpr bool is_heap(_Tp* __first, _Tp* __last) {
    return is_heap_until(__first, __last) == __last;
}

#undef _PRISM_RANGE_CHECK

// Two pointers and one value. An inverted range traps before any write.
// An empty range returns and does not write, even when first is null.
// A null first on a non-empty range traps before any write. Otherwise
// each element in [first, last) is assigned the value. last is not
// written. This is not PROVED and it is not CLEAN.
template <typename _Tp>
constexpr void fill(_Tp* __first, _Tp* __last, const _Tp& __value) {
    if (__last < __first) {
        __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "std::fill last is before first");
        __builtin_trap();
    }
    if (__first == __last) return;
    if (__first == nullptr) {
        __prism_check(0, "PTR-NULL-DEREF", "std::fill null destination");
        __builtin_trap();
    }
    while (__first != __last) {
        *__first = __value;
        ++__first;
    }
}

// Two pointers and one destination. An inverted range traps before any
// read or write. An empty range returns dest and does not read or write,
// even when dest is null. A null dest on a non-empty range traps before
// any write. A dest inside [first, last) traps before any write. That
// scan compares pointers and does not read an element. Otherwise each
// element in [first, last) is copied. last is not written.
// This is not PROVED and it is not CLEAN.
template <typename _Tp>
constexpr _Tp* copy(_Tp* __first, _Tp* __last, _Tp* __dest) {
    if (__last < __first) {
        __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "std::copy last is before first");
        __builtin_trap();
    }
    if (__first == __last) return __dest;
    if (__dest == nullptr) {
        __prism_check(0, "PTR-NULL-DEREF", "std::copy null destination");
        __builtin_trap();
    }
    _Tp* __scan = __first;
    while (__scan != __last) {
        if (__scan == __dest) {
            __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "std::copy dest lies in [first, last)");
            __builtin_trap();
        }
        ++__scan;
    }
    _Tp* __out = __dest;
    while (__first != __last) {
        *__out = *__first;
        ++__first;
        ++__out;
    }
    return __out;
}

// One pointer, a std::size_t count, and one destination. n == 0 returns
// dest and does not read or write, even when either pointer is null. A
// null source with n > 0 traps before any read. A null dest with n > 0
// traps before any write. Otherwise n elements are copied. The next
// element is not written. This is not PROVED and it is not CLEAN.
template <typename _Tp>
constexpr _Tp* copy_n(_Tp* __first, std::size_t __n, _Tp* __dest) {
    if (__n == 0) return __dest;
    if (__first == nullptr) {
        __prism_check(0, "PTR-NULL-DEREF", "std::copy_n null source");
        __builtin_trap();
    }
    if (__dest == nullptr) {
        __prism_check(0, "PTR-NULL-DEREF", "std::copy_n null destination");
        __builtin_trap();
    }
    _Tp* __out = __dest;
    while (__n != 0) {
        *__out = *__first;
        ++__first;
        ++__out;
        --__n;
    }
    return __out;
}

// Three pointers. An inverted range traps before any read or write. An
// empty range returns dest and does not read or write, even when dest is
// null. That check is before the null-destination check. A null dest on a
// non-empty range traps before any write. A dest in (first, last] traps
// before any write. That scan compares pointers and does not read an
// element. Otherwise each element in [first, last) is copied from the back
// into the positions ending at dest. dest is not written. The element at
// last is not read. The result is the start of the written range.
// This is not PROVED and it is not CLEAN.
template <typename _Tp>
constexpr _Tp* copy_backward(_Tp* __first, _Tp* __last, _Tp* __dest) {
    if (__last < __first) {
        __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "std::copy_backward last is before first");
        __builtin_trap();
    }
    if (__first == __last) return __dest;
    if (__dest == nullptr) {
        __prism_check(0, "PTR-NULL-DEREF", "std::copy_backward null destination");
        __builtin_trap();
    }
    _Tp* __scan = __last;
    while (__scan != __first) {
        if (__scan == __dest) {
            __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "std::copy_backward dest lies in (first, last]");
            __builtin_trap();
        }
        --__scan;
    }
    _Tp* __src = __last;
    _Tp* __out = __dest;
    while (__src != __first) {
        --__src;
        --__out;
        *__out = *__src;
    }
    return __out;
}

namespace ranges {

// T is deduced from the array only. The predicate parameter does not take
// part in deduction, so a captureless lambda of const T& converts.
template <typename _Tp>
struct _Prism_count_if_identity {
    using type = _Tp;
};

// How many elements make the predicate true. The bound is at least 1:
// N == 0 does not match this parameter. A null predicate traps before a
// call and before a load. The subscript is below N. The result is a count,
// not a pointer. This is not PROVED and it is not CLEAN.
template <typename _Tp, size_t _Nm>
constexpr __PTRDIFF_TYPE__ count_if(_Tp (&__a)[_Nm],
                                    bool (*__pred)(const typename _Prism_count_if_identity<_Tp>::type&)) {
    if (__pred == nullptr) {
        __prism_check(0, "FUNC-CONTRACT", "std::ranges::count_if null predicate");
        __builtin_trap();
    }
    __PTRDIFF_TYPE__ __n = 0;
    for (size_t __i = 0; __i < _Nm; ++__i) {
        if (__pred(__a[__i])) ++__n;
    }
    return __n;
}

